Shared (client & server)
1) Versioned, bounded serialization

Do: Use explicit bincode options and size limits; add a protocol version field.
Accepts: Deserialize fails on oversized/unknown data; mismatched versions disconnect cleanly.
Refs:

let b = bincode::DefaultOptions::new()
  .with_fixint_encoding()
  .allow_trailing_bytes(false)
  .with_limit(4 * 1024); // tune per message kind



Handshake: include protocol_version: u16 in first reliable message.

2) Message framing for voice (seq/timestamp)

Do: Add {seq: u32, capture_ns: u64} to VoiceData (client→server→clients).
Accepts: Client can reorder, detect loss, and trigger PLC/FEC.
Refs: Keep it in your existing ServerMessage::VoiceData / ClientMessage::VoiceData.

3) Channel QoS audit

Do: Use reliable ordered for control/chat; unreliable sequenced for voice.
Accepts: Out‑of‑date voice frames get dropped automatically; control messages always arrive in order.
Refs: renet::SendType::{ReliableOrdered, UnreliableSequenced} (or closest variants supported).

4) Backpressure & rate limiting

Do: Bound per‑channel/per‑client queues; drop oldest voice when near limit; rate‑limit sends.
Accepts: No unbounded memory growth under spam; voice remains real‑time.
Refs: ConnectionConfig memory caps (already used) + app‑level counters (token bucket).

5) MTU/congestion awareness

Do: Cap voice payload (e.g., ≤ 400 bytes/frame @ 10–20 ms); avoid aggregating > MTU; track send failures.
Accepts: No fragmentation bursts; logs show zero “oversize” drops in steady state.
Refs: Netcode fragments, but still cap your Vec<u8> before serialize.

6) Time sync & latency stats

Do: Add a tiny ping/pong on reliable channel to estimate RTT + clock offset.
Accepts: Client shows smoothed RTT/offset; jitter buffer uses it.
Refs: Send Ping { t0 }, reply Pong { t0, t1_server }, compute on client.

7) Harden username/user_data parsing

Do: Replace panic on long names; validate UTF‑8; trim/normalize.
Accepts: Malformed user_data doesn’t crash; names are bounded & safe.
Refs: String::from_utf8(data).ok().unwrap_or_default(); enforce max len.

8) Metrics & structured logging

Do: Emit counters: msgs/s, voice pkt/s, drops, bytes in/out, queue depth.
Accepts: One DebugStats struct printable on demand; no log spam per packet.
Refs: AtomicU64 + periodic aggregator; tracing crate (swap from log if desired).

Client
9) Robust connect/reconnect with backoff

Do: Exponential backoff on disconnect; surface reason; “rejoin” preserves username.
Accepts: Client recovers from transient network loss without manual restart.
Refs: Backoff via backoff crate or simple capped exponential.

10) Strict send API

Do: Guard send_voice_data with size checks and pacing (frames/tick).
Accepts: No oversize sends; pacing maintains target bitrate.
Refs: Local token bucket per channel.

11) Transport update error handling

Do: Differentiate fatal vs transient errors; queue a “Disconnected(reason)” once.
Accepts: No error floods; clear state transitions (connecting → connected → disconnected).

Server
12) Zero‑copy(ish) broadcast of voice frames

Do: Serialize voice frame once per tick and reuse buffer for broadcast.
Accepts: CPU drops under multi‑client load; no per‑recipient re‑serialize.
Refs: You already serialize once—just avoid extra clones: keep encoded and pass slices.

13) Voice relay flood control

Do: Cap per‑sender voice fps (e.g., ≤ 100pps); drop if queue backed up.
Accepts: One misbehaving client can’t starve others.
Refs: Per‑client moving window counter.

14) Authentication hardening (optional but recommended)

Do: Support ServerAuthentication::Secure with a real key; configurable in YAML.
Accepts: Secure mode works with provided key; unsecure mode gated by config.
Refs: NetcodeServerTransport secure path; store key (32 bytes) and don’t log it.

15) Stats endpoint / admin command

Do: Add a simple text or JSON dump of ServerStats (stdout or TCP admin port).
Accepts: Can fetch live counters without scanning logs.
Refs: Tiny HTTP via tiny_http/rouille or just a CLI command.

Transport / Socket
16) Increase OS UDP buffers & set DSCP (QoS)

Do: Tune SO_RCVBUF/SO_SNDBUF; set DSCP EF (46) for voice if allowed.
Accepts: Fewer drops on bursty networks; optional QoS marking.
Refs: socket2 crate:

use socket2::{Socket, Domain, Type};
sock.set_recv_buffer_size(1<<20)?;
sock.set_send_buffer_size(1<<20)?;
sock.set_tos(46)?; // best-effort if supported

17) Tick pacing & CPU caps

Do: Run server update at fixed tick (e.g., 60 Hz); avoid busy‑spin.
Accepts: Stable CPU usage and latency; timer drift monitored.
Refs: std::thread::sleep with accumulator; or tokio::time::interval if async.

Serialization alternatives (optional)
18) Consider postcard or rkyv for hot paths

Do: Switch voice/control messages to postcard (no_std, small) or rkyv (zero‑copy).
Accepts: Reduced allocs & CPU for (de)serialize; compatibility test passes.
Refs: postcard (to_allocvec/from_bytes), rkyv (Archive, Deserialize).

Small code nits in your current files

Use a single bincode::Options instance (with limits) per module, not bincode::options() ad‑hoc everywhere.

Avoid unwrap() in network paths; map to Disconnected(reason) or warn!.

Username::to_netcode_user_data → return Result<[u8; N], Error> instead of panic!.

Server print_stats: compute deltas since last print, not totals only.

Replace magic IDs (RELIABLE_CHANNEL, VOICE_CHANNEL) with config‑driven values on the client too (you already have it on the server).

